{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774043351,"nonce":"QpWd7VXW6K2K1CreVtsNCa7EpTU=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM70_ei5","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/10","origin":"github"},"title":"Potential solution to missing netmap","message":"# Restoring iptables NETMAP on Teltonika RUTX11 (RutOS 7.13+)\n\n## Background\n\nStarting with **RutOS 7.13** (firmware `RUTX_R_00.07.13`), Teltonika moved the `iptables-mod-nat-extra` kernel module — which provides the `xt_NETMAP` iptables target — out of the base firmware and into an optional package called **\"IPtables NAT extra\"** in the Package Manager. This affects all current firmware versions including the latest stable release (00.07.21.x as of March 2026).\n\nNETMAP itself is fully supported; it just needs to be installed as a package first.\n\n**The catch:** After a firmware upgrade (even with \"keep settings\"), the package must be reinstalled by the Package Restore service. Custom firewall rules in `/etc/firewall.user` execute *before* the package restore completes, so NETMAP rules silently fail on first boot after an upgrade.\n\n### Sources\n\n- Teltonika Community: ['IPtables NAT extra' Package does not reload 'Firewall - Custom Rules'](https://community.teltonika.lt/t/iptables-nat-extra-package-does-not-reload-firewall-custom-rules/13494) (April 2025)\n- Teltonika Wiki: [Overlapping subnets with IPsec solution](https://wiki.teltonika-networks.com/view/Overlapping_subnets_with_IPsec_solution) (updated for firmware 00.07.17.4, lists \"IPtables NAT extra\" as a prerequisite)\n- Teltonika Wiki: [RUTX11 Package Manager](https://wiki.teltonika-networks.com/view/RUTX11_Package_Manager) (firmware 00.07.21.2)\n- Teltonika Wiki: [RUTX11 Firewall](https://wiki.teltonika-networks.com/view/RUTX11_Firewall)\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Install the \"IPtables NAT extra\" Package\n\n1. Log into the RUTX11 WebUI.\n2. Navigate to **System → Package Manager**.\n3. Locate **\"IPtables NAT extra\"** in the package list.\n4. Click the **+** button to install it.\n5. Wait for installation to complete.\n\nIf the router has no internet access, you can download the package `.ipk` file from the [RUTX11 Package Downloads](https://wiki.teltonika-networks.com/view/RUTX11_Package_Downloads) page or the linked package repository, then upload it via **System → Package Manager → Upload**.\n\n### Verify installation via CLI\n\n```bash\n# SSH into the router, then check that the kernel module is available:\nlsmod | grep xt_NETMAP\n\n# Or test directly:\niptables -t nat -L -n | head\n```\n\nIf `xt_NETMAP` doesn't appear in `lsmod`, try loading it manually:\n\n```bash\nmodprobe xt_NETMAP\n```\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Enable Package Restore\n\nThis ensures the package is automatically reinstalled after future firmware upgrades (with \"keep settings\").\n\n1. Navigate to **System → Package Manager**.\n2. Make sure **Package Restore** is set to **Enable**.\n\nThis tells the router to re-download and reinstall all previously installed packages after a firmware upgrade. However, this does **not** solve the boot-order race condition (see Step 4).\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Add NETMAP Rules to Custom Firewall\n\nNavigate to **Network → Firewall → Custom Rules** and add your NETMAP rules. For example, to NAT an entire /24 subnet:\n\n```bash\n# Map local 192.168.1.0/24 to virtual 192.168.3.0/24 for outbound traffic to 192.168.4.0/24\niptables -t nat -I POSTROUTING -s 192.168.1.0/24 -d 192.168.4.0/24 -j NETMAP --to 192.168.3.0/24\n\n# Map inbound traffic from 192.168.4.0/24 back to local 192.168.1.0/24\niptables -t nat -I PREROUTING -s 192.168.4.0/24 -j NETMAP --to 192.168.1.0/24\n```\n\nAdjust the subnets to match your topology. Click **Save** to apply.\n\n\u003e **Source:** The official Teltonika wiki article [Overlapping subnets with IPsec solution](https://wiki.teltonika-networks.com/view/Overlapping_subnets_with_IPsec_solution) uses exactly this pattern for NETMAP rules.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Fix the Boot-Order Race Condition\n\nThis is the critical step. After a firmware upgrade, the custom firewall rules execute on startup *before* the Package Restore service finishes reinstalling `iptables-mod-nat-extra`. The NETMAP rules fail silently because the kernel module isn't loaded yet.\n\n### Option A: Wrapper Script in `/etc/firewall.user`\n\nInstead of placing raw iptables commands in the WebUI Custom Rules, SSH into the router and edit `/etc/firewall.user` directly to include a module-availability check:\n\n```bash\n#!/bin/sh\n\n# Wait for xt_NETMAP module to become available (up to 120 seconds)\n# This handles the race condition after firmware upgrade + package restore\nTIMEOUT=120\nELAPSED=0\nwhile [ $ELAPSED -lt $TIMEOUT ]; do\n    if [ -f /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/xt_NETMAP.ko ] || lsmod | grep -q xt_NETMAP; then\n        break\n    fi\n    sleep 5\n    ELAPSED=$((ELAPSED + 5))\n    logger -t firewall.user \"Waiting for xt_NETMAP module... (${ELAPSED}s)\"\ndone\n\n# Load the module explicitly\nmodprobe xt_NETMAP 2\u003e/dev/null\n\n# Now apply NETMAP rules\niptables -t nat -I POSTROUTING -s 192.168.1.0/24 -d 192.168.4.0/24 -j NETMAP --to 192.168.3.0/24\niptables -t nat -I PREROUTING -s 192.168.4.0/24 -j NETMAP --to 192.168.1.0/24\n\nlogger -t firewall.user \"NETMAP rules applied\"\n```\n\n\u003e **Note:** If you use this approach, remove any duplicate rules from the WebUI Custom Rules page to avoid conflicts.\n\n### Option B: Startup Script in `/etc/rc.local`\n\nAdd a script that waits for the module and then restarts the firewall:\n\n```bash\n#!/bin/sh\n\n# Wait for package restore to complete and xt_NETMAP to be available\n(\n    TIMEOUT=180\n    ELAPSED=0\n    while [ $ELAPSED -lt $TIMEOUT ]; do\n        if lsmod | grep -q xt_NETMAP 2\u003e/dev/null || \\\n           [ -f /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/xt_NETMAP.ko ]; then\n            # Module available, restart firewall to re-apply custom rules\n            sleep 5  # Brief extra delay for stability\n            /etc/init.d/firewall restart\n            logger -t rc.local \"Firewall restarted after xt_NETMAP became available\"\n            exit 0\n        fi\n        sleep 5\n        ELAPSED=$((ELAPSED + 5))\n    done\n    logger -t rc.local \"WARNING: xt_NETMAP not available after ${TIMEOUT}s\"\n) \u0026\n\nexit 0\n```\n\nWith this option, keep your NETMAP rules in the WebUI Custom Rules page as normal. The `rc.local` script simply re-triggers the firewall after the module is ready.\n\n\u003e **Note on rc.local permissions:** As of firmware 00.07.14.3, `/etc/rc.local` executes as root again. Earlier sub-versions of 00.07.14 ran it as `admin`, which broke firewall manipulation. If you're on an affected version, upgrade to at least 00.07.14.3.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Source:** [Teltonika Community confirmation](https://community.teltonika.lt/t/iptables-nat-extra-package-does-not-reload-firewall-custom-rules/13494/2)\n\n---\n\n## Step 5: Verify After Reboot\n\nAfter a reboot (or firmware upgrade), confirm everything is working:\n\n```bash\n# Check module is loaded\nlsmod | grep xt_NETMAP\n\n# Check rules are in place\niptables -t nat -L PREROUTING -n | grep NETMAP\niptables -t nat -L POSTROUTING -n | grep NETMAP\n\n# Check system log for your script's messages\nlogread | grep -E \"firewall.user|rc.local\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## Optional: Policy-Based Routing for NETMAP'd Subnets\n\nIf you're using NETMAP with IPsec or VPN tunnels and need LAN devices to reach the virtual (mapped) subnets, you'll also need routing rules. Via WebUI:\n\n1. Navigate to **Network → Routing → Policy Based Routing**.\n2. Add a new routing table (e.g., ID: `123`, Name: `netmap`).\n3. Add a **Static IPv4 Route** in that table pointing the virtual subnet to the LAN gateway.\n4. Add a **Routing Rule for IPv4** with Priority `1` using your new table.\n\n\u003e **Source:** [Teltonika Wiki — Overlapping subnets with IPsec solution, Routing configuration section](https://wiki.teltonika-networks.com/view/Overlapping_subnets_with_IPsec_solution#Routing_configuration)\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n| Problem | Cause | Fix |\n|---------|-------|-----|\n| NETMAP target not found | Module removed from base firmware in RutOS 7.13+ | Install \"IPtables NAT extra\" from Package Manager |\n| Rules fail after firmware upgrade | Package restore runs after firewall init | Use wrapper script or rc.local to wait for module |\n| rc.local can't restart firewall | Ran as `admin` in some 7.14 builds | Upgrade to ≥ 00.07.14.3 |\n| Virtual subnets unreachable from LAN | Missing routes for mapped addresses | Add policy-based routing entries |","files":null}]}